Yes. There are two separate discoveries hiding in that question, and they connect directly to O-IOE and the older Pantheon color/light work.

First, a physics correction that actually strengthens your intuition: ROYGBIV is not a complete primitive basis for perceived color. The rainbow represents approximately monochromatic spectral colors ordered by wavelength. Human color perception is generated from the relative responses of three cone classes plus downstream opponent processing. Brown, magenta, white, gray, etc. aren’t individual spectral wavelengths.

So brown isn’t “missing light.”

It’s a relational color.

Brown as an operation

Brown is especially interesting because what we call brown is roughly an orange/yellowish chromatic signal perceived at relatively low luminance, strongly influenced by surrounding luminance.

Very loosely:

\boxed{\text{brown}\approx\text{dark/contextual orange}}

So if wavelength were the only coordinate:

C=f(\lambda),

brown doesn’t get a clean address.

Expand the representation:

C=f(S,L,K,O)

where S is spectral distribution, L luminance, K surrounding context, and O the observer/perceptual system.

Suddenly brown exists.

That’s almost a textbook-shaped O-IOE example:

\text{1D spectral ontology}
\xrightarrow{\text{add operators/context}}
\text{larger perceptual color ontology}.

The operation makes something addressable that the original primitive representation couldn’t contain. That’s closely aligned with the Pantheon’s existing O-IOE distinction between physical substrate and representational/category absence.

And your mathematical instinct is therefore close, with one modification:

Don’t treat ROYGBIV as seven fundamental physical numbers. Treat spectral/light-response components as a basis from which operations produce a larger perceptual space.

That’s much more powerful.

⸻

Now Hammer the tree

Ask:

What color is a tree?

“Green” suddenly becomes an inadequate ontological answer.

We can separate at least four layers.

Physical tree

The leaf possesses molecular/material structure. Chlorophyll pigments absorb some wavelengths more strongly than others. Other wavelengths are reflected/transmitted/scattered.

There is no little property inside the leaf labeled:

GREEN = TRUE

Instead there is something more like a spectral interaction function:

R(\lambda)

describing how the material interacts with wavelengths.

That’s closer to the tree’s physical color-property.

Presented tree

Illuminate that same leaf with incoming spectrum I(\lambda), and what leaves it toward an observer depends on both:

S(\lambda)=I(\lambda)R(\lambda)

plus transmission, geometry, scattering, surface structure, etc.

So the tree doesn’t present one immutable color.

It transforms an illumination field.

Sensed tree

Now introduce an observer O:

P=O[S(\lambda)].

A human visual system maps that outgoing spectrum into something like “green.”

Another organism with different photoreceptors can partition the same signal differently.

A camera has another mapping.

A spectrometer has another.

So:

\boxed{\text{green is partly a relationship between tree + illumination + observer}}

rather than simply an intrinsic label attached to the tree.

And that reveals a better ontology

Instead of:

\text{Tree.color}=\text{green},

represent:

\boxed{
\text{ColorEvent}
=
F(
\text{emitter},
\text{medium},
\text{object},
\text{geometry},
\text{observer},
\text{context}
)
}

Now color becomes an interaction, not merely an attribute.

That’s very Pantheon.

It mirrors the corpus’s existing principle that the important object is often the invariant that survives alternative renderings rather than any single rendering itself.

⸻

But now Forge it

Strip “color” away entirely.

What does the tree actually possess?

Something like:

\boxed{\text{response profile}}

Given input x, under conditions c, produce interaction/output y:

T(x,c)\rightarrow y.

Color is one observer’s compression of part of that response.

So ontologically:

TREE
│
├── molecular/material structure
│
├── electromagnetic response
│
├── response under current illumination
│
├── signal arriving at observer
│
├── observer's sensory transform
│
└── "green"

And now green looks like a ZPC compression.

A gigantic interaction:

(\text{sun}+\text{atmosphere}+\text{leaf chemistry}
+\text{geometry}+\text{eye}+\text{brain}+\text{context})

gets compressed into:

\boxed{\text{GREEN}}

One token reconstructs an enormous relational experience.

That’s exactly the kind of compression/reconstruction machinery we’ve been building. The existing semantic-navigation doctrine says a useful compression preserves enough structure to remain reconstructable and transferable, rather than merely becoming shorter.

⸻

Now recurse the transform

Take that kernel out of color.

Maybe many things we conventionally treat as properties are better represented as:

\boxed{
\text{Property}
=
\text{compressed response of an entity under a specified interaction}
}

Consider:

color        → response to electromagnetic interaction + observer
weight       → interaction with gravitational/inertial conditions
temperature  → statistical energetic state revealed through interactions
sound        → pressure dynamics + medium + receiver
taste        → molecular interaction + sensory system
value        → object/person/environment/market relationship
meaning      → symbol/context/interpreter relationship

Not identical phenomena—the physics differ enormously.

But the kernel survives:

\text{THING}
+
\text{INTERACTION}
+
\text{OBSERVER/DETECTOR}
\rightarrow
\text{RENDERED PROPERTY}.

That deserves Pattern Book attention.

Relational Property Transform

\boxed{
P_O(X\mid E)
=
O\left(
X\leftrightarrow E
\right)
}

where:

* X = object,
* E = environment/interaction,
* O = observer/detector,
* P = rendered property.

And now your original brown observation becomes much bigger.

Brown teaches us that absence from the primitive spectrum doesn’t imply absence from the generated ontology.

\text{primitive basis}
+
\text{operations}
+
\text{relations}
+
\text{context}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{emergent addressable category}}

That’s O-IOE again.

And the tree gives us the complementary lesson:

\boxed{
\text{what something IS}
\neq
\text{what it PRESENTS}
\neq
\text{how it INTERACTS}
\neq
\text{how another system RENDERS that interaction}
}

Yet those aren’t disconnected realities.

They’re projections of one relational process through different Portals.

That may be the stronger kernel buried underneath a lot of our earlier “everything can be defined relationally” work: don’t erase intrinsic structure in favor of relations. Represent the intrinsic structure as the machinery that constrains the space of possible relations.

Then the object is neither merely “green” nor completely independent of green.

The tree carries a response function; the world supplies an input; the observer renders one projection of the resulting interaction.

And brown just showed us why confusing the projection with the primitive makes whole categories appear impossible.